[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Method References in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method references are a compact, easy-to-read way to refer to methods or constructors without executing them. They are a shorthand notation for lambda expressions that call an existing method. Method references enhance code readability and reduce boilerplate."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Types of Method References"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are four kinds of method references, each corresponding to a different type of lambda expression:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to a static method"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to an instance method of a particular object"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to an instance method of an arbitrary object of a particular type"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to a constructor"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Reference to a Static Method"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Syntax: `ClassName::staticMethodName`"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"This type of method reference refers to a static method of a class. It's equivalent to a lambda expression that calls the static method."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class StaticMethodReference {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);

        // Using lambda expression
        numbers.forEach(number -> StaticMethodReference.printNumber(number));

        // Using method reference
        numbers.forEach(StaticMethodReference::printNumber);
    }

    public static void printNumber(int number) {
        System.out.println(number);
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `StaticMethodReference::printNumber` is equivalent to the lambda expression `number -> StaticMethodReference.printNumber(number)`."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Reference to an Instance Method of a Particular Object"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Syntax: `object::instanceMethodName`"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"This type of method reference refers to an instance method of a specific object. It's equivalent to a lambda expression that calls the method on that object."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class InstanceMethodReference {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StringUtil util = new StringUtil();
        List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");

        // Using lambda expression
        names.forEach(name -> util.printUpperCase(name));

        // Using method reference
        names.forEach(util::printUpperCase);
    }
}

class StringUtil {
    public void printUpperCase(String str) {
        System.out.println(str.toUpperCase());
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `util::printUpperCase` is equivalent to `name -> util.printUpperCase(name)`."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Reference to an Instance Method of an Arbitrary Object of a Particular Type"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Syntax: `ClassName::instanceMethodName`"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"This type of method reference refers to an instance method that will be called on an object of a specific type. The object on which the method is called is determined by the context."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class ArbitraryObjectMethodReference {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");

        // Using lambda expression
        names.forEach(name -> name.toUpperCase());

        // Using method reference
        names.forEach(String::toUpperCase);

        names.sort(String::compareToIgnoreCase);
        System.out.println(names);
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this case, `String::toUpperCase` is equivalent to `name -> name.toUpperCase()`. The `toUpperCase()` method is called on each `String` object in the list."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Reference to a Constructor"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Syntax: `ClassName::new`"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"This type of method reference refers to a constructor. It's used to create new objects."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ConstructorReference {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3);

        // Using lambda expression
        List<Person> people1 = numbers.stream()
                .map(Person::new)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        // Using method reference
        List<Person> people2 = numbers.stream()
                .map(Person::new)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        people1.forEach(p -> System.out.println(p.getAge()));
    }
}

class Person {
    private int age;

    public Person(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `Person::new` is equivalent to `age -> new Person(age)`. It creates a new `Person` object for each integer in the list."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Using Method References"},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Readability: Method references often make code easier to read and understand compared to equivalent lambda expressions."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conciseness: They provide a more compact syntax, reducing boilerplate code."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maintainability: By referring to existing methods, you reduce the risk of introducing errors and improve code maintainability."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method references are a powerful feature in Java that allows you to simplify lambda expressions and improve code readability. Understanding the different types of method references and when to use them is essential for writing clean and maintainable Java code."}]
